{"nodes": [{"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_product_strategy_and_features_product_strategy___feature_discipline", "label": "Product Strategy & Feature Discipline", "file_type": "document", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/product-strategy-and-features.md", "source_location": "document", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_product_strategy_and_features_feature_requests_from_cancellations_don_t_buy_features", "label": "Feature requests from cancellations don't buy features", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/product-strategy-and-features.md", "source_location": "section: Feature requests from cancellations don't buy features", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Three auto shops canceled RepareCar over missing boleto (bank-slip) issuance \u2014 request logged in Linear with name and date, 20+ in the same queue, oldest since June. But cancellation doesn't buy a feature: the ICP profile is defined, and boleto sits in a deliberately-not-doing package. Support is semi-automated (AI triages, human on novel questions, both open Linear tickets; a daily Claude task prioritizes). Bugs jump the queue; anything new defaults to parking lot."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_product_strategy_and_features_building_is_the_comfort_zone__distribution_is_the_game", "label": "Building is the comfort zone; distribution is the game", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/product-strategy-and-features.md", "source_location": "section: Building is the comfort zone; distribution is the game", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Founders choose to build because selling hurts \u2014 Richard spent 90% of his time perfecting a delivery product one year before iFood existed; it died beautiful. Humane raised ~US$240M, ex-Apple founders, gorgeous hardware; returns outpaced sales by summer 2024 and HP took the assets for US$116M. In survival phase you do both jobs and almost always pick building \u2014 your comfort zone \u2014 pretending the next feature will make selling easier. Exercise: how many months of runway does your next feature cost?"}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_product_strategy_and_features_94__of_features_are_filler___pendo_s_data_and_what_to_do_about_it", "label": "94% of features are filler \u2014 Pendo's data and what to do about it", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/product-strategy-and-features.md", "source_location": "section: 94% of features are filler \u2014 Pendo's data and what to do about it", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Pendo's analysis across ~2,500 companies' apps found roughly 6% feature adoption in the average product; even top-10% products reached only 15.6%. That leaves about 94% rarely or never used in the average product (84.4% even in the top decile), creating low activation, churn, a larger attack surface, and higher sustaining cost/complexity. Some features sell without being used, but that doesn't explain the gap."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_product_strategy_and_features_pmf_is_step_8_in_a_10_step_sequence___founders_start_there_too_early", "label": "PMF is step 8 in a 10-step sequence \u2014 founders start there too early", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/product-strategy-and-features.md", "source_location": "section: PMF is step 8 in a 10-step sequence \u2014 founders start there too early", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Sequence: (1) Define the ICP \u2014 does it really exist, or is it who you wish it were? (2) Surface the ICP's pains and desires \u2014 real? verified where, with how many? (3) Understand awareness level \u2014 knows the pain? the solution? yours? (4) Slice niche/sub-niche \u2014 their particularities, do they exclude each other? (5) Size TAM of niche and sub \u2014 enough market, and does enough remain after niching? (6) Actively decide who the solution serves \u2014 can you stand out there? (7) Validate the thesis \u2014 the ICP's pain solved in a way that pays; are they paying? (8) Reach PMF \u2014 does the product stick? do users recommend it unprompted? does the business sustain itself without you pushing? (9) Fix inefficiencies in acquisition, activation, retention \u2014 always leaks; measured where? (10) Scale."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_product_strategy_and_features_feature_creep___fiturite___is_a_business_cost__not_just_a_ux_one", "label": "Feature creep (\"fiturite\") is a business cost, not just a UX one", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/product-strategy-and-features.md", "source_location": "section: Feature creep (\"fiturite\") is a business cost, not just a UX one", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The Homer Simpson car problem: every unsatisfied user request implemented as a feature eventually makes the product hard to operate, steepens the learning curve, and worsens experience through cognitive overload and choice paradox. It's bad for the business too \u2014 you spend more on dev for features serving few users, more on maintenance, eventually whole teams for barely-needed areas. Prune based on: who are our most strategic users, what are they using, what's unused that I can abstract, what aligns with the vision."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_product_strategy_and_features_maturing_the_product_means_doing_the_same_thing_better__not_adding_power_user_features", "label": "Maturing the product means doing the same thing better, not adding power-user features", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/product-strategy-and-features.md", "source_location": "section: Maturing the product means doing the same thing better, not adding power-user features", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Technical founders keep densifying the product for heavy users, making it expensive, complex, and hard for the average user \u2014 a Boeing 747 cockpit. Maturing means doing better what the product already does: smoother flows, bug fixes, attention direction. Hidden risk: a power-user product has a smaller top of funnel, so you must charge more \u2014 you changed markets (TAM/SAM changed) and price must follow. The founder then reads \"product is bad\" when really ICP isn't adjusted. Adjust copy (more executive, less tool-like), visuals (show the result, less product internals), and detail care (high-paying ICP tolerates AI-slop design less)."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_product_strategy_and_features_run_every_feature_through_a_two_layer__swiss_knife_filter__before_building", "label": "Run every feature through a two-layer \"Swiss Knife filter\" before building", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/product-strategy-and-features.md", "source_location": "section: Run every feature through a two-layer \"Swiss Knife filter\" before building", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A feature you ship stays forever and charges rent forever, so the right question isn't \"is this good?\" but \"does it deserve the permanent cost it imposes on the product?\""}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_product_strategy_and_features_feature_adoption_is_a_design_problem__not_a_communication_problem", "label": "Feature adoption is a design problem, not a communication problem", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/product-strategy-and-features.md", "source_location": "section: Feature adoption is a design problem, not a communication problem", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Shipping a feature doesn't make it discovered; users move through their habitual path and never see what they aren't looking for."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_product_strategy_and_features_compute_your_swiss_knife_index_to_expose_feature_creep", "label": "Compute your Swiss Knife Index to expose feature creep", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/product-strategy-and-features.md", "source_location": "section: Compute your Swiss Knife Index to expose feature creep", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A product's worth is measured by features actually used, not features shipped; a bloated product is expensive to sustain and hard to sell, not rich."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_product_strategy_and_features_design_the_attention_hierarchy_to_direct_behavior__not_just_organize_info", "label": "Design the attention hierarchy to direct behavior, not just organize info", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/product-strategy-and-features.md", "source_location": "section: Design the attention hierarchy to direct behavior, not just organize info", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A product that organizes delivers access; a product that directs delivers activation \u2014 and the visual hierarchy decides which the user gets."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_product_strategy_and_features_ground_feature_discipline_in_the_academic_feature_fatigue_research", "label": "Ground feature discipline in the academic feature-fatigue research", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/product-strategy-and-features.md", "source_location": "section: Ground feature discipline in the academic feature-fatigue research", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Past a cognitive-load threshold, the subjective evaluation of a product doesn't stay neutral \u2014 it declines into frustration, confusion, and task abandonment, directly hitting CAC and LTV."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_product_strategy_and_features_past_the_optimal_feature_count__a_technically_bigger_product_becomes_functionally_worse", "label": "Past the optimal feature count, a technically bigger product becomes functionally worse", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/product-strategy-and-features.md", "source_location": "section: Past the optimal feature count, a technically bigger product becomes functionally worse", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The relationship between feature count and perceived utility is non-linear: there's an optimal point, after which each added feature reduces perceived utility while raising sustaining cost and the learning curve."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_product_strategy_and_features_focus_on_your_core__trying_to_be__all_in_one__dilutes_your_value_proposition", "label": "Focus on your core; trying to be \"all-in-one\" dilutes your value proposition", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/product-strategy-and-features.md", "source_location": "section: Focus on your core; trying to be \"all-in-one\" dilutes your value proposition", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Chasing a bigger TAM by going generic destroys retention of your heavy users without converting new ones \u2014 the same roadmap mistake in cars and in software."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_product_strategy_and_features__richardrx___2026_08_10", "label": "@richardrx \u00b7 2026-08-10", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/product-strategy-and-features.md", "source_location": "link: @richardrx \u00b7 2026-08-10", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "reference_url": "https://x.com/richardrx/status/2086931878251057479"}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_product_strategy_and_features__richardrx___2026_07_21", "label": "@richardrx \u00b7 2026-07-21", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/product-strategy-and-features.md", "source_location": "link: @richardrx \u00b7 2026-07-21", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "reference_url": "https://x.com/richardrx/status/2079575881115226453"}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_product_strategy_and_features__richardrx___2026_07_17", "label": "@richardrx \u00b7 2026-07-17", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/product-strategy-and-features.md", "source_location": "link: @richardrx \u00b7 2026-07-17", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "reference_url": "https://x.com/richardrx/status/2078079228717764886"}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_product_strategy_and_features__richardrx___2026_07_09", "label": "@richardrx \u00b7 2026-07-09", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-ce